,{&quot;content&quot;:&quot;Selecting a name for your non-profit organizati=
on is an important step in establishing your brand identity. Here's a step-=
by-step guide to help you choose a name and obtain a trademark:\n\n**Choosi=
ng a Name:**\n\n1. Brainstorm a list of potential names that reflect your m=
ission, values, and purpose. Consider names that are memorable, easy to spe=
ll, and easy to pronounce.\n2. Conduct a preliminary search to ensure the n=
ame isn't already in use by another organization. You can use online direct=
ories, such as the IRS's Exempt Organizations Business Master File (EOBMF) =
or the Secretary of State's database in your state, to search for existing =
non-profit organizations with similar names.\n3. Check if the desired web d=
omain and social media handles are available. This will help you ensure con=
sistency across all your online platforms.\n4. Narrow down your list to a f=
ew options and test them with your target audience, stakeholders, and poten=
tial donors to gather feedback.\n\n**Obtaining a Trademark:**\n\n1. Conduct=
 a comprehensive trademark search: Once you've selected a name, conduct a t=
horough search of the United States Patent and Trademark Office's (USPTO) d=
atabase to ensure the name doesn't infringe on an existing trademark. You c=
an use the USPTO's Trademark Electronic Search System (TESS) database to se=
arch for similar trademarks.\n2. Determine the type of trademark you need: =
Non-profit organizations typically file for a service mark (SM) rather than=
 a trademark (TM), as they provide services rather than goods.\n3. File a t=
rademark application: If your search doesn't reveal any conflicting tradema=
rks, you can file a trademark application with the USPTO. You'll need to pr=
ovide:\n\t* The name and address of your organization\n\t* A clear descript=
ion of the services you'll be providing\n\t* A specimen of the mark (e.g., =
a logo or wordmark)\n\t* The filing fee (currently $225-$675 per class of g=
oods or services)\n4. Wait for the USPTO to review your application: The re=
view process typically takes 6-12 months. If your application is approved, =
your trademark will be published in the Official Gazette, and you'll have s=
ix months to respond to any oppositions.\n5. Maintain your trademark: After=
 your trademark is registered, you'll need to file periodic maintenance doc=
uments and fees to keep your registration active.\n\n**Additional Considera=
tions:**\n\n* Register your trademark with the state: In addition to federa=
l registration, you may also want to register your trademark with your stat=
e's Secretary of State office.\n* Consider registering a DBA (Doing Busines=
s As) name: If your organization's name is different from its legal name, y=
ou may need to file a DBA registration with your state or local government.=
\n* Use the TM or SM symbol: Until your trademark is registered, you can us=
e the TM or SM symbol to indicate that you're claiming trademark rights. On=
ce your trademark is registered, you can use the symbol.\n\nRemember to con=
sult with an attorney specializing in intellectual property law to ensure y=
ou're following the correct procedures and protecting your organization's b=
