hich simplifies to $x \\geq \\frac{5}{2}$.\n\\[\n2x - 5 =3D 3x + 1\n\\Right=
arrow -x =3D 6\n\\Rightarrow x =3D -6\n\\]\nHowever, $x =3D -6$ does not sa=
tisfy $x \\geq \\frac{5}{2}$. Thus, no solutions exist in this case.\n\n**C=
ase 2**: When $2x - 5 &lt; 0$, which implies $x &lt; \\frac{5}{2}$.\n\\[\n-=
(2x-5) =3D 3x + 1\n\\Rightarrow -2x + 5 =3D 3x + 1\n\\Rightarrow 5x =3D 4\n=
\\Rightarrow x =3D \\frac{4}{5}\n\\]\nWe then check if $x =3D \\frac{4}{5}$=
 meets $x &lt; \\frac{5}{2}$ which is true.\n\nSubstituting $x =3D \\frac{4=
}{5}$ back into the original equation to verify,\n\\[\n|2(\\frac{4}{5}) - 5=
| =3D 3(\\frac{4}{5}) + 1\n\\Rightarrow |-\\frac{1}{5}| =3D \\frac{12}{5} +=
