    "content": "In Java, `break`, `continue`, and `return` are three differ=
ent statements that control the flow of a program's execution. Here's a bri=
ef overview of each:\n\n1. **Break Statement:**\n   - The `break` statement=
 is used to exit a loop (for, while, do-while, or switch statement) immedia=
tely.\n   - When a `break` statement is encountered, the loop is terminated=
, and the program control passes to the statement that follows the loop.\n =
  - It can also be used with a label to exit a specific loop in a nested lo=
op scenario.\n\n   Example:\n   ```java\nfor (int i =3D 0; i &lt; 10; i++) =
{\n    if (i =3D=3D 5) {\n        break;\n    }\n    System.out.println(i);=
\n}\n```\n   This code will print numbers from 0 to 4.\n\n2. **Continue Sta=
tement:**\n   - The `continue` statement is used to skip the current iterat=
ion of a loop and move to the next iteration.\n   - When a `continue` state=
ment is encountered, the current iteration is skipped, and the program cont=
rol passes to the next iteration of the loop.\n   - Like `break`, `continue=
` can also be used with a label to skip a specific loop in a nested loop sc=
enario.\n\n   Example:\n   ```java\nfor (int i =3D 0; i &lt; 10; i++) {\n  =
  if (i =3D=3D 5) {\n        continue;\n    }\n    System.out.println(i);\n=
}\n```\n   This code will print all numbers from 0 to 9 except 5.\n\n3. **R=
eturn Statement:**\n   - The `return` statement is used to exit a method an=
d return control to the caller method.\n   - When a `return` statement is e=
ncountered, the method is terminated, and the program control passes back t=
o the caller method.\n   - The `return` statement can also be used to retur=
n a value from a method.\n\n   Example:\n   ```java\npublic static void mai=
n(String[] args) {\n    System.out.println(add(2, 3));\n}\n\npublic static =
int add(int a, int b) {\n    return a + b;\n}\n```\n   This code will print=
 the sum of 2 and 3, which is 5.\n\nIn summary:\n\n- `break` exits a loop o=
r switch statement.\n- `continue` skips the current iteration of a loop and=
 moves to the next iteration.\n- `return` exits a method and returns contro=
l to the caller method, potentially returning a value.",
    "role": "assistant"
  }
